<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ============================================================================
         SCRUM-107: PAI RECORDS THE CREDITORS IT COULD NOT FIND. IT DOES NOT INVENT
         THEM IN SOMEBODY ELSE'S ACCOUNT MASTER.

         PAI used to answer an absent creditor account with INSERT INTO account,
         carrying hardcoded values taken from a toolkit SAMPLE file: product FNBRF,
         acc_type CACC, branch 250205, ucn 100000000000, client_id 2, app_no mirroring
         the account number, and a balance of 999999999.99 chosen, in its own words,
         "generous so caps pass post-init".

         That is not reference data. It is a sentinel with a fixed shape, and it was
         being written into the relation the payments validator reads. PTV's account
         tier now fails closed, so the two together made a loop: PTV rejects an unknown
         creditor once, PAI mints it with a billion-rand balance, and every later
         arrival to that creditor clears BOTH the existence tier and the cap tier
         unconditionally, for ever. A control that a downstream stage can switch off,
         one account at a time, is not a control.

         The write therefore moves here, to a relation PAI owns, named for what the row
         means. Nothing about it presupposes where the account master ends up living:
         this table records an OBSERVATION PAI made ("this creditor was not in the
         master when arrival X was initialised"), which stays true and stays PAI's
         under every option.

         PAI still READS the master to make that observation. That read is a separate
         question and is deliberately untouched here: a context that cannot control
         writes to a relation does not own it, and PAI now writes none.

         UNGUARDED, matching 001-pai.xml and the v1 baselines elsewhere in the estate:
         PAI is the only creator, so a tableExists MARK_RAN guard could never fire, and
         a guard that cannot fire is not free. It would silently SKIP the create on the
         day the assumption stopped holding, turning a loud startup failure into a
         table that is quietly missing.
         ============================================================================ -->

    <changeSet id="003-unknown-creditor-pai" author="dcre">
        <createTable tableName="unknown_creditor">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- Names the account master's own column, so the no-host-prefix rule
                 exempts it: the host here is unknown_creditor. -->
            <column name="account_number" type="VARCHAR(34)">
                <constraints nullable="false" unique="true"
                             uniqueConstraintName="uq_unknown_creditor_account_number"/>
            </column>
            <!-- The arrival during which this creditor was FIRST observed to be absent.
                 The row is insert-once (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING), so a later arrival
                 crediting the same account does not overwrite the first sighting. -->
            <column name="arrival_id" type="UUID">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
